---
name: ptt-inbox
description: "Show what is new in the records for this session's holder since it last looked (a subordinate's status or done report, an amendment, a note, a review or decision on its request, the close of its own task) and what waits on it. Use for: anything new, what needs me, before starting a step. Argument: none or a holder name."
---

# Inbox

The command, or an AtE naming these records, approves the records this skill
writes (it writes none); other work needs AtE (rules file).

1. `<records>/bin/ptt inbox` (the holder bound to this session), or `ptt
   inbox <name>` with the holder name this session got from `/ptt-take` or
   `/ptt-resume`. Looking moves the holder's marker: the next look shows
   only what came after.
2. Report each line with a suggested next step: read a changed `status.md`
   or note and say what it means for your work; read an amendment (`ptt
   show <N>`); answer a review (`/ptt-pr-draft-reply`); record a
   decided request's outcome in your status; settle what waits on you
   (`/ptt-close`, `/ptt-leave approve`). Act only with the user's command or
   AtE.
3. When the hold has ended (closed, leave approved, or freed by the
   supervisor), the inbox says so once to the session last bound to it:
   record nothing more as that holder, and offer the user to clear the
   session.

The installed prompt hook runs before each user message in Claude Code
and, once trusted, Codex. It adds what is new to the conversation; treat
such lines like this report. In Codex, review/trust `.codex/hooks.json`
(`/hooks` in the CLI); use `$ptt-inbox` manually if the hook is unavailable.
A holder that is not bound to the session (`/ptt-resume`, `$ptt-resume` in
Codex) gets nothing from the hook. An idle session gets no notification
until its next user message.
